  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to employ a hybrid optimization strategy that combines binary search with continuous optimization. This method leverages the periodic structure of optimal step functions by using binary representations near key decision thresholds (0.3 and 0.7) to escape shallow local optima. The core innovation is to introduce a dual-layer regret-based loss function that prioritizes progress on the most constraining lags while preserving global optimality. This approach differs from the previous work by maintaining binary feasibility at stochastic decision points, enhancing exploration of the binary hypercube, and introducing automated target adjustment based on lag-dependent violation patterns.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Representation: Hybrid representation with 8-dimensional decision vector s, where 4 bits represent binary decisions and the remaining dimensions store smooth relaxations\n- Variables: Continuous version: h(x) \u2208 [0,1] for all x; Binary decisions enforced via fixed threshold points\n- Objective: Minimize max_k \u222b_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n- Constraints: Integral condition \u222b_0^2 h(x) dx = 1; 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n- Smoothed objective: S_l = (max_k corr_k + sum_i |h[i] - s[i]|) / (1 + l)\n- Projection: Binary rounding for h(x) = 0.5 + 0.5*(sign(h(x)-0.4) for quantile-based rounding\n- Feasibility: crafted via constrained gradient descent with exponential smoothing\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nInitialize n = min(280, initial_grid_size or 140) if parent exists else 280\n- Handle parent input if available covering universe scaling [0,2]\n- Grid validation: if n_params < 280, scale existing parent across 0-2 interval\n- Define core search space h: size n, [0,1] continuous, with integral 140\n- Objective function: \n  def compute_overlap(h):\n    corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n    return dx * np.max(corr), dx * np.mean(corr)\n\n- Budget allocation:\n  \u2119hasedn=280n=280\n  Initial scaling: with seed and binary injection\n  Two-layer regret conditioning:\n    Layer1: stochastic binary injection at thresholds 0.3/0.7\n    Layer2: gradient smoothing with entropy penalty\n  Score selection: maximize overlap minimization score\n\n- Optimization loop:\n  while time_remaining > 10 seconds:\n    1. Sample k top-ranked binary injection points\n    2. Apply quantized-friendly projection Q\n    3. Compute gradients on both continuous and binary components\n    4. Use dual-layer backpropagation targeting lags with highest violation\n    5. Update rule: h[i] += \u03b7 * \u2207 + \u03b6 * I(h[i] near decision threshold)\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Primary grid mutations: \n  - Adaptive bitwise randomization\n  - Smart threshold injection (quantile-based: 0.3 and 0.7)\n  - Soft reset mechanism based on highest-violating lag\n- Hyperparameter validation:\n  \u03b7 \u2208 [0.05, 0.2] tunable by exponential cooling during failure detection\n  \u03b6 \u2208 [0.01, 0.5] adaptive via cost gradient analysis\n  window_size \u2208 [5, 20] move to binary update halting\n- Schedule tuning:\n  initial boost: start with coarse grain seeding then slowly honing to binary-constrained islands\n  automatic threshold adjustment: based on average lag violation\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Estimated timing profile:\n  Projection phase: 5-60 seconds (depends on grid size)\n  Optimization kernel: 800-1000 seconds for n=280\n  Validation and crossover: \n    - Use binary decision compression to maintain fidelity across grid sizes\n    - Project using SPA method\n\n- Termination conditions and checks:\n  - Timeout appended: revert to last known feasible solution\n  - NaN/overflow checks on all gradient components\n  - Budget\ud558\uc2e0 \ub9d0\uc500 \ucc28\ud6c4 \ubc1b\ub3c4\ub85d \ud558\uaca0\uc2b5\ub2c8\ub2e4. \ud655\uc778 \ubd80\ud0c1\ub4dc\ub9bd\ub2c8\ub2e4.",
